::ENG3_U01_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence is correctly capitalized? {
=The phrase "unsteady light" suggests uncertainty.#Correct. The sentence begins with a capital letter, and the quoted phrase remains lowercase because it is a phrase from the text.
~the phrase "unsteady light" suggests uncertainty.#This sentence needs a capital letter at the beginning.
~The Phrase "unsteady light" suggests uncertainty.#The word Phrase should not be capitalized in the middle of the sentence.
~The phrase "unsteady light" Suggests uncertainty.#The verb suggests should not be capitalized in the middle of the sentence.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision fixes the run-on sentence? Draft: The narrator pauses at the doorway the pause shows hesitation. {
~The narrator pauses at the doorway the pause shows hesitation.#This keeps the run-on because two complete ideas are joined without punctuation.
=The narrator pauses at the doorway, and the pause shows hesitation.#Correct. The comma and coordinating conjunction join the complete ideas clearly.
~The narrator pauses at the doorway, the pause shows hesitation.#This is a comma splice because a comma alone joins two complete sentences.
~The narrator pauses at the doorway the pause, shows hesitation.#The punctuation is misplaced and does not separate the complete ideas.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence uses quotation marks correctly for a phrase from a text? {
~The phrase quiet refusal" shows resistance.#The opening quotation mark is missing.
~The phrase "quiet refusal shows resistance.#The closing quotation mark is missing.
=The phrase "quiet refusal" shows resistance.#Correct. The exact phrase is enclosed in quotation marks.
~The "phrase quiet refusal" shows resistance.#The quotation marks include the writer's wording instead of only the text phrase.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision corrects this fragment? Fragment: Although the image suggests hope. {
~Although the image suggests hope.#This remains a fragment because although makes the thought incomplete.
~Although hope suggests the image.#This is grammatically awkward and does not express the intended analysis.
~The image although suggests hope.#This places although incorrectly.
=The image suggests hope although the surrounding scene is bleak.#Correct. The sentence contains a complete idea and uses although clearly.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence is clearest and most correct? {
~The author uses imagery it makes the scene tense.#This is a run-on sentence.
~The author uses imagery, it makes the scene tense.#This is a comma splice.
=The author uses imagery to make the scene tense.#Correct. This sentence is concise, complete, and clear.
~The author uses, imagery to make the scene tense.#The comma incorrectly separates the verb from its object.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which correction best fixes the comma splice? Draft: The phrase suggests pride, it also reveals fear. {
~The phrase suggests pride, it also reveals fear.#This repeats the comma splice.
~The phrase suggests pride it also reveals fear.#This removes the comma but creates a run-on.
~The phrase suggests pride, also revealing, fear.#This punctuation is confusing and interrupts the meaning.
=The phrase suggests pride, but it also reveals fear.#Correct. The comma and conjunction connect the contrasting ideas clearly.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision keeps the meaning and improves the sentence boundary? Draft: The line is short it creates emphasis. {
=The line is short, which creates emphasis.#Correct. The revision connects the line's structure to its effect.
~The line is short it creates emphasis.#This remains a run-on sentence.
~The line is short, it creates emphasis.#This is a comma splice.
~The line is short creates emphasis.#This removes a needed connector and makes the sentence unclear.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence correctly introduces quoted evidence? {
~The narrator says "we waited" which shows tension.#This sentence needs a comma after says to introduce the quotation.
=The narrator says, "we waited," which shows tension.#Correct. The quotation is introduced and punctuated clearly.
~The narrator says, "we waited which shows tension.#The quotation is not closed.
~The narrator says, we waited, which shows tension.#The exact words from the text need quotation marks.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence avoids unnecessary capitalization? {
~The Symbol suggests memory because it returns often.#Symbol should not be capitalized unless it begins a sentence or is part of a proper noun.
~The symbol Suggests memory because it returns often.#Suggests should not be capitalized in the middle of the sentence.
=The symbol suggests memory because it returns often.#Correct. The sentence uses standard capitalization.
~the symbol suggests memory because it returns often.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision best corrects the sentence? Draft: Because the speaker changes tone at the end. {
~Because the speaker changes tone at the end.#This is a fragment because because makes the thought incomplete.
=The speaker changes tone at the end, which signals a shift in attitude.#Correct. The revision turns the fragment into a complete analytical sentence.
~Because the speaker changes tone at the end, which signals.#This is still incomplete.
~The speaker because changes tone at the end.#This places because incorrectly and makes the sentence unclear.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence correctly joins evidence and explanation? {
~The repeated word "still" shows endurance it suggests the speaker has not given up.#This is a run-on.
~The repeated word "still" shows endurance, it suggests the speaker has not given up.#This is a comma splice.
=The repeated word "still" shows endurance because it suggests the speaker has not given up.#Correct. Because connects the evidence to the explanation.
~The repeated word still shows "endurance because" it suggests the speaker has not given up.#The quotation marks are misplaced and distort the meaning.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which correction is best for this draft? Draft: the final image suggests renewal because the field is described as "green again." {
~the final image suggests renewal because the field is described as "green again."#The sentence still begins with a lowercase letter.
~The final image suggests renewal because the Field is described as "green again."#Field should not be capitalized.
~The final image suggests renewal because the field is described as green again.#The exact phrase should remain in quotation marks.
=The final image suggests renewal because the field is described as "green again."#Correct. Capitalization and quotation marks are used clearly.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence has the clearest punctuation? {
=The phrase "locked door" suggests exclusion, not safety.#Correct. The comma helps separate the contrast at the end of the sentence.
~The phrase "locked door" suggests, exclusion not safety.#The comma incorrectly separates the verb from its object.
~The phrase, "locked door" suggests exclusion not safety.#The comma after phrase is unnecessary and interrupts the subject.
~The phrase "locked door suggests exclusion, not safety.#The quotation is not closed.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision corrects the sentence boundary? Draft: The evidence is powerful it changes the reader's view. {
~The evidence is powerful it changes the reader's view.#This keeps the run-on.
=The evidence is powerful because it changes the reader's view.#Correct. The connector explains why the evidence is powerful.
~The evidence is powerful, it changes the reader's view.#This is a comma splice.
~The evidence powerful because it changes the reader's view.#This removes a needed verb.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence is a complete sentence? {
~When the author shifts from fear to confidence.#This is a fragment.
~Because the detail reveals the character's doubt.#This is a fragment.
=The detail reveals the character's doubt through the word "hesitated."#Correct. This is a complete analytical sentence.
~Although the line creates a serious tone.#This is a fragment.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision best fixes this comma splice? Draft: The symbol appears twice, it becomes more important. {
~The symbol appears twice, it becomes more important.#This repeats the comma splice.
~The symbol appears twice it becomes more important.#This creates a run-on.
~The symbol appears, twice it becomes more important.#The comma is misplaced and does not fix the boundary.
=The symbol appears twice, so it becomes more important.#Correct. The comma and so show the relationship between the ideas.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence correctly punctuates the quoted word? {
=The word "bitter" creates a harsh tone.#Correct. The quoted word is enclosed cleanly and the sentence is complete.
~The word bitter" creates a harsh tone.#The opening quotation mark is missing.
~The word "bitter creates a harsh tone.#The closing quotation mark is missing.
~The "word bitter" creates a harsh tone.#The quotation marks include the writer's word instead of only the text word.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision best improves clarity? Draft: The author repeats the phrase it makes the warning stronger. {
~The author repeats the phrase it makes the warning stronger.#This is a run-on.
=The author repeats the phrase, which makes the warning stronger.#Correct. The revision connects the action to its effect.
~The author repeats, the phrase which makes the warning stronger.#The comma separates the verb from its object.
~The author repeats the phrase which, makes the warning stronger.#The comma interrupts the phrase and makes the sentence unclear.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence uses standard conventions? {
~the imagery suggests isolation because the street is empty.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
~The imagery suggests isolation, because, the street is empty.#The commas around because are unnecessary and confusing.
=The imagery suggests isolation because the street is empty.#Correct. The sentence is complete, clear, and conventionally punctuated.
~The imagery suggests, isolation because the street is empty.#The comma incorrectly separates the verb from its object.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision corrects the fragment? Fragment: Since the final sentence sounds uncertain. {
~Since the final sentence sounds uncertain.#This remains a fragment.
~The final sentence since sounds uncertain.#This word order is incorrect.
~Since uncertain final sentence sounds.#This is not a clear sentence.
=The final sentence sounds uncertain, so the ending feels unresolved.#Correct. The sentence expresses a complete analytical idea.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence best corrects the error? Draft: The phrase "empty chair" suggests absence it makes the room feel lonely. {
=The phrase "empty chair" suggests absence, which makes the room feel lonely.#Correct. The revision connects the evidence to the explanation with correct punctuation.
~The phrase "empty chair" suggests absence it makes the room feel lonely.#This keeps the run-on.
~The phrase "empty chair" suggests absence, it makes the room feel lonely.#This is a comma splice.
~The phrase empty chair suggests "absence which" makes the room feel lonely.#The quotation marks are misplaced and the meaning is unclear.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence correctly handles capitalization and quoted evidence? {
~the phrase "unanswered call" suggests distance.#This sentence needs a capital letter at the beginning.
=The phrase "unanswered call" suggests distance.#Correct. The sentence begins correctly and quotes the exact phrase.
~The Phrase "unanswered call" suggests distance.#Phrase should not be capitalized in the middle of the sentence.
~The phrase "unanswered call" Suggests distance.#Suggests should not be capitalized in the middle of the sentence.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision best fixes the run-on sentence? Draft: The character looks away this shows discomfort. {
~The character looks away this shows discomfort.#This remains a run-on.
~The character looks away, this shows discomfort.#This is a comma splice.
=The character looks away, showing discomfort.#Correct. The revision combines the ideas clearly.
~The character looks away showing, discomfort.#The comma is misplaced and interrupts the meaning.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which correction best improves this analytical sentence? Draft: The closing line creates suspense because it ends with "before dawn" {
~The closing line creates suspense because it ends with "before dawn"#The sentence needs ending punctuation.
~The closing line creates suspense, because, it ends with "before dawn."#The commas around because are unnecessary and confusing.
~The closing line creates suspense because it ends with before dawn.#The quoted phrase should stay in quotation marks.
=The closing line creates suspense because it ends with "before dawn."#Correct. The sentence is complete and punctuated clearly.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence is the clearest corrected version? Draft: The image shows growth it also shows struggle. {
~The image shows growth it also shows struggle.#This remains a run-on sentence.
~The image shows growth, it also shows struggle.#This is a comma splice.
=The image shows both growth and struggle.#Correct. This concise revision preserves the meaning and removes the boundary error.
~The image shows, both growth and struggle.#The comma incorrectly interrupts the verb phrase.
}
